      "claim": "Medieval English peasants (serfs/villeins) were legally bound to the land and lacked the legal capacity to freely negotiate terms or exit the relationship with their lord.",
      "evidence": "Historical records, including manorial court rolls and the Statute of Labourers (1351), demonstrate that serfdom restricted mobility and that labor services (corvée) were non-negotiable legal obligations enforced by fines and imprisonment.",
